{
  "analysis": "Market read: the last session was broadly positive for equity holdings (1d returns +0.3% to +1.6% across most positions) while bonds (A09) fell -0.79%; there is no news feed and the portfolio is only -1.14% from peak, so no defensive drawdown actions are triggered. The key structural fact in the forecasts is dispersion in interval width, since medians carry no directional skill: the 5-day 80% intervals range from 3.6% (A02) and 3.9% (A09) up to 11.1% (A01), 9.8% (A14), 9.3% (A07), 8.1% (A13), 7.9% (A12) and 7.3% (A05). The baseline already exploits this, and the current portfolio is already tilted the right way: 19.1% cash, zero in the four widest-interval assets (A12, A01, A07, A14) and minimal gold, concentrated in the narrow-interval assets (A02, A08, A09, A06, A10). My decision: keep that structure, but deploy part of the excess cash into the two most predictable assets — A02 (narrowest equity interval, 3.6%, low 10% vol) and A09 (narrowest interval overall, 3.9%, defensive ballast) — taking them ~2.2-2.4pp above current, funded from cash, leaving cash at 14.5% (still well above the 10% floor). I deliberately do NOT add to the wide-interval assets despite some positive medians (e.g., A13, A12), because medians have no demonstrated skill; energy's strong 20d run (+11% to +12%) is not a reason to chase given 21% vol and wide intervals. Group exposures stay within caps: us_broad 23.1%, financials 18.0%, tech_growth 5.0%, energy 8.0%, health 5.9%. All increases vs baseline are <= 4.8pp (limit 15pp), all weights long-only, sum to 1 with cash.",
